  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Cooler temperatures and periods of valley rain and mountain\n   snow Saturday evening through Monday\n\n-  High pressure bringing stable conditions, temperatures slightly\n   above normal through midweek\n\n-  Low confidence for more unsettled weather later next week\n\n\nRadar returns early this afternoon indicate scattered showers\nacross northwest Montana and Clearwater County in Idaho. \nSatellite imagery indicates a closed circulation of low pressure\nover the Pacific Ocean just off the coast of the Olympic\nPeninsula. This feature is sending moisture into the Northern \nRockies resulting in light precipitation and cloud cover. That \nlow will continue to spin over the coast into the weekend before \ndeepening and moving onshore on Saturday night. In the meantime, \nexpect enough moisture to reach the Northern Rockies for partly \ncloudy to cloudy skies and daytime temperatures to reach just a \nfew degrees above normal. \n\nBy the time the Pacific low gets inland on Saturday, it will\nfollow a familiar theme and move south, bringing most of its\nforcing with it. There will, however, be enough moisture and lift\nfor precipitation focused on north-central Idaho and southwest \nMontana, as well as the mountains along the Continental Divide, on\nSaturday night through Monday. Snow levels will be around \n5000-6000 feet, which would be low enough for a couple inches of \nsnow on Lost Trail Pass and Gilmore Summit by Monday morning. \n\nHigh pressure builds for midweek, allowing for stable, warm\nconditions. The stability could mean fog for the Tuesday morning\ncommute in the valleys of western Montana such as the Bitterroot,\nMissoula, Mission, and Flathead valleys. Ensemble guidance is\nhinting at more unsettled weather for later next, but there is \nstill considerable spread in the solutions, so confidence is quite\nlow at this time.",
